IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Act 1959 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what the air passenger meets: which regime
-- governs the flight (Part IA, the 1999 Montreal Convention, Schedule 1A; Part II, the Warsaw Convention as amended at The
-- Hague, Schedule 2; Part III, the Warsaw Convention unamended, Schedule 1; Part IV, domestic and other carriage, s 27);
-- the carrier's liability for death or bodily injury (Montreal Arts 17(1), 20, 21; Warsaw Arts 17, 20; s 28), for baggage
-- (Montreal Art 17(2)-(3); Warsaw Art 18; s 29) and for delay (Montreal Art 19; Warsaw Art 19); the limits (Montreal Arts
-- 21, 22, 25; Warsaw Arts 22, 25; ss 11A, 21A, 31); complaints (Montreal Art 31; Warsaw Art 26; s 30); the two years to sue
-- (Montreal Art 35; Warsaw Art 29; s 34); who an action for a passenger's death is for (ss 5(2)-(3), 9D, 12, 24, 35);
-- what is not deducted from damages (ss 9G, 15, 38); contracting out (Montreal Art 26; Warsaw Art 23; s 32); contributory
-- negligence (ss 9H, 16, 39); and turning SDRs into dollars (s 9).
--
-- SEAT: the air passenger (and, for a passenger's death, the family member who claims). "My flight injured me, or my bag
-- was lost or my flight was late: is the airline liable, under which regime, and up to how much? By when must I complain,
-- and by when must I sue?" Provisions addressed to the court or the Minister are encoded as what they mean for the
-- passenger: how the court must reduce damages, which notice is evidence of which countries are parties.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 30, compilation date 17 June 2021 (register id C2021C00236), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/C1959A00002.txt (Schedule 1A, the 1999 Montreal Convention, at lines 587-828).
--
-- FIGURES. The SDR figures are the deposited text's: 100 000 SDRs (Art 21), 4 150 SDRs (Art 22(1)) and 1 000 SDRs (Art
-- 22(2)) of the 1999 Montreal Convention as set out in Schedule 1A; 260 000 SDRs (ss 11A, 21A); 480 000 SDRs and $925 000,
-- $3 000 and $300 (s 31). The Act's definition of "the 1999 Montreal Convention" (s 5(1)) takes in any Article 24 revision
-- of the limits that has become effective, and ss 9C, 11A, 21A and 31 let regulations prescribe or index higher figures.
-- Neither a revision nor the regulations are deposited, so the figures here are the text's; a revision, a regulation or
-- indexation would change the answers (NOTES.md finding 2).
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Part IA ss 9J-9L, Part II ss 17-19, Part III ss 22-25, Part IIIA (the Guadalajara Convention, actual
-- carriers), Part IIIC (the Montreal No. 4 Convention), Part IVA (carriers' insurance), s 40 (ticket regulations), s 41
-- (cargo), s 42 (stowaways), cargo throughout, the Conventions' documentation and jurisdiction articles, successive and
-- combined carriage, servants' and agents' own liability (s 33; Montreal Art 30). A question that reaches a gap answers
-- `not covered`.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the passenger's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Act 1959 -- the air passenger`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- s 27(1): Part IV covers carriage between States, to or within a Territory, or to or from Australia. Carriage within
    -- one State is left to State law (WA has its own Act).
    `a flight that begins and ends in one State, with no landing outside it, is not carriage this Act governs (s 27): State law applies`
    -- Conventions Art 1(1); s 27(1): "commercial transport operations"
    `this flight is neither carriage for reward (or free carriage by an air transport undertaking) under a Convention nor commercial carriage under Part IV (s 27)`
    -- s 27(1)(d) needs one end in Australia; a journey wholly outside Australia is governed here only by a Convention
    `no Convention applies to this journey, and Part IV covers only carriage within Australia or between Australia and another country (s 27)`
    -- Part IV ss 28, 29: death, injury and baggage only
    `Part IV imposes no liability for delay: a claim for a late domestic flight lies, if at all, under the contract or other law, not this Act`
    -- Warsaw Arts 18, 22(3): the liability for objects the passenger takes charge of is not encoded
    `the Warsaw Conventions' liability for objects the passenger takes charge of (unchecked baggage) is not encoded`
    -- Montreal Art 17(3) has no counterpart for baggage in the Warsaw Conventions or Part IV
    `only the 1999 Montreal Convention (Art 17(3)) lets a passenger treat checked baggage as lost after 21 days`
    -- ss 9G, 15, 38 list the payments not to be deducted; anything else is for the general law
    `the Act lists the payments not to be deducted (ss 9G, 15, 38); whether another payment reduces damages is for the general law`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `Which law governs my flight -- s 27, s 9B, s 11, s 21, Art 1 of each Convention`

-- Where the contract of carriage puts the place of departure and the place of destination. Art 1(2) of each Convention:
-- "international carriage means any carriage in which ... the place of departure and the place of destination ... are
-- situated either within the territories of two States Parties, or within the territory of a single State Party if there
-- is an agreed stopping place within the territory of another State". s 27(1)(a)-(d) and (3) for Part IV.
DECLARE `Where my journey begins and ends` IS ONE OF
    `in two different Australian States`
    `in Australia, with one or both ends in a Territory`
    `in one State, with no landing outside that State`
    `in one State, with a landing in another State or a Territory`
    `one end in Australia and the other in another country`
    `both in Australia, with an agreed stopping place in another country`
    `both outside Australia`

DECLARE `Regime` IS ONE OF
    -- Part IA, s 9B: the 1999 Montreal Convention (Schedule 1A) has the force of law
    `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)`
    -- Part II, s 11: the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague (Schedule 2)
    `the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague (Part II)`
    -- Part III, s 21: the Warsaw Convention unaffected by the Hague Protocol (Schedule 1)
    `the Warsaw Convention without the Hague Protocol (Part III)`
    -- Part IV, s 27(1)(a)-(c), (3): a "domestic carrier" (s 26)
    `Part IV, as domestic carriage`
    -- Part IV, s 27(1)(d): between Australia and another country, no Convention applying
    `Part IV, as international carriage no Convention covers`

GIVEN `where` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the journey is international carriage under the Conventions` IF
    CONSIDER `where`
    WHEN `one end in Australia and the other in another country`        THEN TRUE
    WHEN `both in Australia, with an agreed stopping place in another country` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `both outside Australia`                                       THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 27(1)(a)-(c) and s 27(3): the journeys a "domestic carrier" (s 26) flies.
GIVEN `where` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the journey is domestic carriage under Part IV` IF
    CONSIDER `where`
    WHEN `in two different Australian States`                        THEN TRUE
    WHEN `in Australia, with one or both ends in a Territory`        THEN TRUE
    WHEN `in one State, with a landing in another State or a Territory` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- The order follows Art 55 of the 1999 Montreal Convention ("This Convention shall prevail over any rules which apply to
-- international carriage by air" between States Parties to it and the Warsaw instruments), then Part II before Part III
-- (s 20(1): Part III is the Warsaw Convention "unaffected by the Hague Protocol"), then Part IV, which applies only to
-- carriage "not being carriage to which the 1999 Montreal Convention, the Warsaw Convention, the Hague Protocol ... applies"
-- (s 27(1)). Whether a country is a party is a fact (ss 9K, 18, 22 make the Minister's notice evidence of it).
GIVEN `where` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends`
      `parties to Montreal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `parties to Hague` IS A BOOLEAN
      `parties to Warsaw` IS A BOOLEAN
      `carried for reward` IS A BOOLEAN
      `commercial operator` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Regime`
`the regime for` MEANS
    IF `the journey is international carriage under the Conventions` `where` AND `carried for reward` AND `parties to Montreal`
    THEN `answered` `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)`
    ELSE IF `the journey is international carriage under the Conventions` `where` AND `carried for reward` AND `parties to Hague`
         THEN `answered` `the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague (Part II)`
         ELSE IF `the journey is international carriage under the Conventions` `where` AND `carried for reward` AND `parties to Warsaw`
              THEN `answered` `the Warsaw Convention without the Hague Protocol (Part III)`
              ELSE IF `where` EQUALS `in one State, with no landing outside that State`
                   THEN `not covered` `a flight that begins and ends in one State, with no landing outside it, is not carriage this Act governs (s 27): State law applies`
                   ELSE IF (NOT `commercial operator`)
                        THEN `not covered` `this flight is neither carriage for reward (or free carriage by an air transport undertaking) under a Convention nor commercial carriage under Part IV (s 27)`
                        ELSE IF `the journey is domestic carriage under Part IV` `where`
                             THEN `answered` `Part IV, as domestic carriage`
                             ELSE IF `where` EQUALS `one end in Australia and the other in another country`
                                  THEN `answered` `Part IV, as international carriage no Convention covers`
                                  ELSE `not covered` `no Convention applies to this journey, and Part IV covers only carriage within Australia or between Australia and another country (s 27)`

@export Which law governs the airline's liability to me for my flight: the Montreal Convention, a Warsaw Convention, or Part IV of the Act?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket (the contract of carriage) puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage (or it is otherwise for reward), or you are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward (or, for a flight to or from Australia, in the course of trade and commerce between Australia and another country)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Regime`
`Which law governs my flight` MEANS
    `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`

§§ `Death or bodily injury -- Montreal Arts 17(1), 20, 21; Warsaw Arts 17, 20, 22, 25; ss 11A, 21A, 28, 31`

DECLARE `Is the carrier liable for my injury` IS ONE OF
    -- Montreal Art 17(1), Art 21(1)-(2)
    `yes: the carrier is liable whatever the cause, and cannot exclude or limit its liability up to 100 000 SDRs; above that it escapes only if it proves the damage was not due to its negligence or was solely due to a third party (Montreal Arts 17(1), 21)`
    -- Warsaw Art 17, Art 20(1)
    `yes, unless the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all necessary measures to avoid the damage or that it was impossible to take them (Warsaw Arts 17, 20)`
    -- s 28
    `yes: the carrier is liable for damage from an accident on board or in embarking or disembarking (s 28)`
    -- Montreal Art 17(1); Warsaw Art 17; s 28
    `no: the carrier is liable only for an accident that took place on board the aircraft or in the course of embarking or disembarking`

@export Is the airline liable for my death or bodily injury from an accident on my flight?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
      `the accident happened on board or while embarking or disembarking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the accident that injured you took place on board the aircraft or in the course of getting on or off it
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is the carrier liable for my injury`
`Is the carrier liable for my injury` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN
        IF (NOT `the accident happened on board or while embarking or disembarking`)
        THEN `answered` `no: the carrier is liable only for an accident that took place on board the aircraft or in the course of embarking or disembarking`
        ELSE CONSIDER regime
             WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)` THEN `answered` `yes: the carrier is liable whatever the cause, and cannot exclude or limit its liability up to 100 000 SDRs; above that it escapes only if it proves the damage was not due to its negligence or was solely due to a third party (Montreal Arts 17(1), 21)`
             WHEN `the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague (Part II)` THEN `answered` `yes, unless the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all necessary measures to avoid the damage or that it was impossible to take them (Warsaw Arts 17, 20)`
             WHEN `the Warsaw Convention without the Hague Protocol (Part III)` THEN `answered` `yes, unless the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all necessary measures to avoid the damage or that it was impossible to take them (Warsaw Arts 17, 20)`
             OTHERWISE `answered` `yes: the carrier is liable for damage from an accident on board or in embarking or disembarking (s 28)`

-- A limit is in the unit the provision names. SDRs are converted into dollars at judgment (s 9); gold francs at the rate on
-- the day damages are ascertained (s 23) or by their gold value (Hague Art 22(5)).
DECLARE `Limit` IS ONE OF
    `no limit`
    `a limit in SDRs`                HAS `SDRs`         IS A NUMBER
    `a limit in Australian dollars`  HAS `dollars`      IS A NUMBER
    `a limit in gold francs`         HAS `francs`       IS A NUMBER
    -- Montreal Art 22(2); Warsaw Art 22(2): a special declaration of interest in delivery
    `the sum I declared when I checked the baggage in`

GIVEN `the Act's figure` IS A NUMBER
      `the contract's figure` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the higher of` MEANS
    IF `the contract's figure` GREATER THAN `the Act's figure` THEN `the contract's figure` ELSE `the Act's figure`

-- Montreal Art 21: no limit up to 100 000 SDRs, and none above it unless the carrier proves (2)(a) or (b); Art 25: the
-- carrier may stipulate higher limits. ss 11A(1), 21A(1): an Australian international carrier's limit is 260 000 SDRs, or
-- the higher number the contract of carriage specifies (the regulations under paragraph (b) are not deposited). Warsaw-Hague
-- Art 22(1): 250 000 francs; Warsaw Art 22(1): 125 000 francs; Art 25 of each: no limit for wilful misconduct (Warsaw) or an
-- act or omission "done with intent to cause damage or recklessly and with knowledge that damage would probably result"
-- (Hague). s 31(1), (1AA): a domestic carrier's limit is $925 000; (1A), (1B): another Part IV carrier's is 480 000 SDRs; in
-- each, a higher figure the contract specifies. Part IV has no exception for wilful misconduct.
GIVEN `regime` IS A `Regime`
      `the carrier proved no negligence or sole third-party fault` IS A BOOLEAN
      `intent or recklessness` IS A BOOLEAN
      `Australian international carrier` IS A BOOLEAN
      `contract limit` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Limit`
`the injury limit under` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regime`
    WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)` THEN
        IF `the carrier proved no negligence or sole third-party fault`
        THEN `a limit in SDRs` (`the higher of` 100000 `contract limit`)
        ELSE `no limit`
    WHEN `the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague (Part II)` THEN
        IF `intent or recklessness`
        THEN `no limit`
        ELSE IF `Australian international carrier`
             THEN `a limit in SDRs` (`the higher of` 260000 `contract limit`)
             ELSE `a limit in gold francs` 250000
    WHEN `the Warsaw Convention without the Hague Protocol (Part III)` THEN
        IF `intent or recklessness`
        THEN `no limit`
        ELSE IF `Australian international carrier`
             THEN `a limit in SDRs` (`the higher of` 260000 `contract limit`)
             ELSE `a limit in gold francs` 125000
    WHEN `Part IV, as domestic carriage` THEN `a limit in Australian dollars` (`the higher of` 925000 `contract limit`)
    WHEN `Part IV, as international carriage no Convention covers` THEN `a limit in SDRs` (`the higher of` 480000 `contract limit`)

@export What is the most the airline can be made to pay for my death or bodily injury?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
      `the airline proves the damage was not its fault or was solely a third party's` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the airline proves that the damage was not due to its (or its servants' or agents') negligence or other wrongful act, or was solely due to a third party's (Montreal Art 21(2); the court decides)
      `the damage was caused with intent or recklessly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is proved that the airline or its servant or agent, acting in the course of employment, caused the damage by wilful misconduct, or with intent to cause damage or recklessly knowing damage would probably result (Warsaw Art 25; the court decides)
      `the airline is an Australian international carrier` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the airline is designated or authorised by Australia under a bilateral arrangement to operate scheduled international services, or is an Australian operator of a permitted non-scheduled international flight
      `the higher limit my ticket states` IS A NUMBER @desc The limit of liability your contract of carriage states, if it is higher than the Act's (in Australian dollars for a domestic flight, in SDRs otherwise); 0 if it states none
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Limit`
`The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN `answered` (`the injury limit under` regime `the airline proves the damage was not its fault or was solely a third party's` `the damage was caused with intent or recklessly` `the airline is an Australian international carrier` `the higher limit my ticket states`)

§§ `Baggage -- Montreal Arts 17(2)-(3), 22(2), 22(5); Warsaw Arts 18, 20, 22, 25; ss 29, 31(2)-(4)`

DECLARE `Which baggage` IS ONE OF
    -- "checked baggage" (Montreal), "registered baggage" (Warsaw; s 26)
    `checked baggage`
    -- "unchecked baggage, including personal items" (Montreal Art 17(2)); s 26 "baggage" (b)
    `baggage I kept with me`

DECLARE `Is the carrier liable for my baggage` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the carrier is liable for checked baggage destroyed, lost or damaged on board or while in its charge, whatever the cause (Montreal Art 17(2))`
    `no, to the extent the damage resulted from the inherent defect, quality or vice of the baggage (Montreal Art 17(2))`
    `yes: for baggage you kept with you, the damage resulted from the carrier's fault or that of its servants or agents (Montreal Art 17(2))`
    `no: for baggage you kept with you, the carrier is liable only if the damage resulted from its fault or that of its servants or agents (Montreal Art 17(2))`
    `yes, because the occurrence took place during the carriage by air and the carrier has not proved that it took all necessary measures (Warsaw Arts 18, 20; s 29(1))`
    `no: the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all necessary measures, or that it was impossible to take them (Warsaw Art 20; s 29(1))`
    `no: the occurrence did not take place during the carriage by air (Montreal Art 17(2); Warsaw Art 18; s 29(2)-(3))`

@export Is the airline liable for my baggage that was destroyed, lost or damaged?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
      `which baggage` IS A `Which baggage` @desc Whether it was baggage you checked in, or baggage and personal items you kept with you
      `it happened during the carriage by air` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what destroyed, lost or damaged it happened on board, or (for checked baggage) while the airline had charge of it; for a domestic flight, the airline's charge ends 12 hours after arrival if the bag was then available for collection
      `the damage came from a defect in the baggage itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the damage resulted from an inherent defect, quality or vice of the baggage (for example, it was already failing)
      `the airline proves it took all necessary measures` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the airline proves that it and its servants and agents took all necessary measures to avoid the damage, or that it was impossible to take them (the court decides)
      `the damage was the airline's fault` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For baggage you kept with you: whether the damage resulted from the fault of the airline or its servants or agents
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is the carrier liable for my baggage`
`Is the airline liable for my baggage` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN
        CONSIDER regime
        WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)` THEN
            CONSIDER `which baggage`
            WHEN `checked baggage` THEN
                IF (NOT `it happened during the carriage by air`)
                THEN `answered` `no: the occurrence did not take place during the carriage by air (Montreal Art 17(2); Warsaw Art 18; s 29(2)-(3))`
                ELSE IF `the damage came from a defect in the baggage itself`
                     THEN `answered` `no, to the extent the damage resulted from the inherent defect, quality or vice of the baggage (Montreal Art 17(2))`
                     ELSE `answered` `yes: the carrier is liable for checked baggage destroyed, lost or damaged on board or while in its charge, whatever the cause (Montreal Art 17(2))`
            WHEN `baggage I kept with me` THEN
                IF `the damage was the airline's fault`
                THEN `answered` `yes: for baggage you kept with you, the damage resulted from the carrier's fault or that of its servants or agents (Montreal Art 17(2))`
                ELSE `answered` `no: for baggage you kept with you, the carrier is liable only if the damage resulted from its fault or that of its servants or agents (Montreal Art 17(2))`
        WHEN `Part IV, as domestic carriage` THEN `the Part IV or Warsaw baggage answer` `it happened during the carriage by air` `the airline proves it took all necessary measures`
        WHEN `Part IV, as international carriage no Convention covers` THEN `the Part IV or Warsaw baggage answer` `it happened during the carriage by air` `the airline proves it took all necessary measures`
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `which baggage`
            WHEN `checked baggage` THEN `the Part IV or Warsaw baggage answer` `it happened during the carriage by air` `the airline proves it took all necessary measures`
            WHEN `baggage I kept with me` THEN `not covered` `the Warsaw Conventions' liability for objects the passenger takes charge of (unchecked baggage) is not encoded`

-- Warsaw Art 18(1)-(2), Art 20(1); s 29(1)-(3) (s 29 covers both registered and other baggage).
GIVEN `during the carriage by air` IS A BOOLEAN
      `all necessary measures` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is the carrier liable for my baggage`
`the Part IV or Warsaw baggage answer` MEANS
    IF (NOT `during the carriage by air`)
    THEN `answered` `no: the occurrence did not take place during the carriage by air (Montreal Art 17(2); Warsaw Art 18; s 29(2)-(3))`
    ELSE IF `all necessary measures`
         THEN `answered` `no: the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all necessary measures, or that it was impossible to take them (Warsaw Art 20; s 29(1))`
         ELSE `answered` `yes, because the occurrence took place during the carriage by air and the carrier has not proved that it took all necessary measures (Warsaw Arts 18, 20; s 29(1))`

-- Montreal Art 22(2): 1 000 SDRs "for each passenger" for destruction, loss, damage or delay, unless a special declaration
-- of interest was made and any supplementary sum paid; Art 22(5): paragraphs 1 and 2 do not apply to an act or omission
-- "done with intent to cause damage or recklessly and with knowledge that damage would probably result"; Art 25: a higher
-- stipulated limit. Warsaw Art 22(2): 250 francs per kilogram of registered baggage, or the declared sum; Art 22(3): 5 000
-- francs for objects the passenger takes charge of; Art 25. s 31(2), (2A): $3 000 where the baggage "is, or includes,
-- registered baggage"; s 31(3), (4): $300 for baggage other than registered baggage; a higher contract figure in each.
@export What is the most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
      `the claim includes checked baggage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is, or includes, baggage you checked in (rather than only baggage you kept with you)
      `the weight of my checked baggage in kilograms` IS A NUMBER @desc The total weight in kilograms of the checked bags concerned (used only under a Warsaw Convention)
      `I declared a higher value when I checked it in` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when you handed over the checked baggage, you made a special declaration of interest in delivery and paid any supplementary sum asked
      `the damage was caused with intent or recklessly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is proved that the airline or its servant or agent caused the damage by wilful misconduct, or with intent to cause damage or recklessly knowing damage would probably result (the court decides)
      `the higher limit my ticket states` IS A NUMBER @desc The baggage limit your contract of carriage states, if higher than the Act's (in Australian dollars for a domestic flight, in SDRs otherwise); 0 if none
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Limit`
`The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN
        CONSIDER regime
        WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)` THEN
            IF `the damage was caused with intent or recklessly`
            THEN `answered` `no limit`
            ELSE IF `the claim includes checked baggage` AND `I declared a higher value when I checked it in`
                 THEN `answered` `the sum I declared when I checked the baggage in`
                 ELSE `answered` (`a limit in SDRs` (`the higher of` 1000 `the higher limit my ticket states`))
        WHEN `Part IV, as domestic carriage` THEN `answered` (`a limit in Australian dollars` (`the Part IV baggage figure` `the claim includes checked baggage` `the higher limit my ticket states`))
        WHEN `Part IV, as international carriage no Convention covers` THEN `answered` (`a limit in Australian dollars` (`the Part IV baggage figure` `the claim includes checked baggage` `the higher limit my ticket states`))
        OTHERWISE
            IF `the damage was caused with intent or recklessly`
            THEN `answered` `no limit`
            ELSE IF `the claim includes checked baggage`
                 THEN IF `I declared a higher value when I checked it in`
                      THEN `answered` `the sum I declared when I checked the baggage in`
                      ELSE `answered` (`a limit in gold francs` (250 TIMES `the weight of my checked baggage in kilograms`))
                 ELSE `answered` (`a limit in gold francs` 5000)

-- s 31(2)-(4): the baggage limits apply to every Part IV carrier, in dollars, not only to a domestic carrier.
GIVEN `includes registered baggage` IS A BOOLEAN
      `contract figure` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Part IV baggage figure` MEANS
    IF `includes registered baggage`
    THEN `the higher of` 3000 `contract figure`
    ELSE `the higher of` 300 `contract figure`

-- Montreal Art 17(3): "If the carrier admits the loss of the checked baggage, or if the checked baggage has not arrived at
-- the expiration of twenty-one days after the date on which it ought to have arrived, the passenger is entitled to enforce
-- against the carrier the rights which flow from the contract of carriage." Art 52: calendar days.
@export From what day may I treat my missing checked bag as lost and claim for it?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
      `the day my bag ought to have arrived` IS A DATE @desc The day your checked bag ought to have arrived at your destination
      `the airline has admitted it is lost` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the airline has admitted that the bag is lost
      `the day the airline admitted it` IS A DATE @desc The day the airline admitted the loss (if it has)
GIVETH AN `Answer` DATE
`From when may I treat my checked bag as lost` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN
        CONSIDER regime
        WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)` THEN
            IF `the airline has admitted it is lost` AND `the day the airline admitted it` LESS THAN (`the day my bag ought to have arrived` PLUS 22)
            THEN `answered` `the day the airline admitted it`
            ELSE `answered` (`the day my bag ought to have arrived` PLUS 22)
        OTHERWISE `not covered` `only the 1999 Montreal Convention (Art 17(3)) lets a passenger treat checked baggage as lost after 21 days`

§§ `Delay -- Montreal Arts 19, 22(1), 22(5); Warsaw Arts 19, 20, 22(1), 25`

DECLARE `My claim for delay` IS ONE OF
    -- Montreal Art 19 second sentence; Warsaw Art 20(1)
    `no claim: the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all the measures that could reasonably be required (Montreal) or all necessary measures (Warsaw) to avoid the damage, or that it was impossible to take them`
    `a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` HAS `the limit` IS A `Limit`

-- Montreal Art 19; Art 22(1): "the liability of the carrier for each passenger is limited to 4 150 Special Drawing Rights",
-- not applying under Art 22(5) (intent or recklessness). Warsaw Art 19 makes the carrier liable for delay; the only limit
-- for passengers is Art 22(1), the per-passenger sum (ss 11A and 21A apply only to injury or death). Part IV: none.
@export Can I claim from the airline for the delay to my flight, and up to how much?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
      `the airline proves it took the measures required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the airline proves that it and its servants and agents took all measures that could reasonably be required (or, under Warsaw, all necessary measures) to avoid the damage, or that it was impossible to take them (the court decides)
      `the delay was caused with intent or recklessly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is proved that the airline or its servant or agent caused the damage with intent, or recklessly knowing damage would probably result, or by wilful misconduct (the court decides)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `My claim for delay`
`Can I claim for the delay to my flight` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN
        IF `the airline proves it took the measures required`
        THEN CONSIDER regime
             WHEN `Part IV, as domestic carriage` THEN `not covered` `Part IV imposes no liability for delay: a claim for a late domestic flight lies, if at all, under the contract or other law, not this Act`
             WHEN `Part IV, as international carriage no Convention covers` THEN `not covered` `Part IV imposes no liability for delay: a claim for a late domestic flight lies, if at all, under the contract or other law, not this Act`
             OTHERWISE `answered` `no claim: the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all the measures that could reasonably be required (Montreal) or all necessary measures (Warsaw) to avoid the damage, or that it was impossible to take them`
        ELSE CONSIDER regime
             WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)` THEN
                 IF `the delay was caused with intent or recklessly`
                 THEN `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` `no limit`)
                 ELSE `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` (`a limit in SDRs` 4150))
             WHEN `the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague (Part II)` THEN
                 IF `the delay was caused with intent or recklessly`
                 THEN `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` `no limit`)
                 ELSE `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` (`a limit in gold francs` 250000))
             WHEN `the Warsaw Convention without the Hague Protocol (Part III)` THEN
                 IF `the delay was caused with intent or recklessly`
                 THEN `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` `no limit`)
                 ELSE `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` (`a limit in gold francs` 125000))
             OTHERWISE `not covered` `Part IV imposes no liability for delay: a claim for a late domestic flight lies, if at all, under the contract or other law, not this Act`

§§ `Complaints and time limits -- Montreal Arts 31, 35; Warsaw Arts 26, 29; ss 30, 34`

DECLARE `What happened to my baggage` IS ONE OF
    `it was damaged`
    `part of an item was lost or destroyed`
    `a whole item was lost or destroyed`
    `it was delayed`

DECLARE `My complaint deadline` IS ONE OF
    -- the written complaint must be given or sent by the end of this day
    `complain to the carrier in writing by` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    -- Montreal Art 31 and Warsaw Art 26 name damage and delay only; s 30(2) does not reach delay
    `the Act sets no time for a complaint about this`

-- Montreal Art 31(2): "in the case of damage ... within seven days from the date of receipt in the case of checked baggage
-- ... In the case of delay ... within twenty-one days from the date on which the baggage ... have been placed at his or her
-- disposal"; (4) otherwise no action lies, save fraud. Warsaw-Hague Art 26(2): seven days (damage), twenty-one days
-- (delay). Warsaw Art 26(2): three days (damage), fourteen days (delay). s 30(2): (a) injury to registered baggage or loss of
-- part of an item, three days after receipt; (b) loss of a whole item of registered baggage, twenty-one days from the date
-- it should have been placed at the passenger's disposal; (c) baggage other than registered baggage, three days from the
-- date the carriage ended; (3) the court may give leave where it is just and equitable "by reason of special circumstances".
-- This encoding reads the loss of part of an item as "damage" under the Conventions (NOTES.md finding 6).
@export By when must I complain in writing to the airline about my baggage?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
      `which baggage` IS A `Which baggage` @desc Whether it was baggage you checked in, or baggage and personal items you kept with you
      `what happened` IS A `What happened to my baggage` @desc What happened to the baggage
      `the day I received it` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the baggage (or what was left of the item) from the airline
      `the day it was or should have been given to me` IS A DATE @desc The day the baggage was placed at your disposal (for a delay), or should have been (for a lost item)
      `the day my journey ended` IS A DATE @desc The day your carriage by air ended
GIVETH AN `Answer` `My complaint deadline`
`By when must I complain about my baggage` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN
        CONSIDER regime
        WHEN `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)` THEN `answered` (`the Convention complaint deadline` `which baggage` `what happened` `the day I received it` `the day it was or should have been given to me` 7 21)
        WHEN `the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague (Part II)` THEN `answered` (`the Convention complaint deadline` `which baggage` `what happened` `the day I received it` `the day it was or should have been given to me` 7 21)
        WHEN `the Warsaw Convention without the Hague Protocol (Part III)` THEN `answered` (`the Convention complaint deadline` `which baggage` `what happened` `the day I received it` `the day it was or should have been given to me` 3 14)
        OTHERWISE `answered` (`the Part IV complaint deadline` `which baggage` `what happened` `the day I received it` `the day it was or should have been given to me` `the day my journey ended`)

-- Montreal Art 31(1)-(2) and Warsaw Art 26(1)-(2) are about baggage delivered to "the person entitled to delivery", which is
-- checked (registered) baggage; read so, they set no time for baggage the passenger kept (NOTES.md finding 6).
GIVEN `which baggage` IS A `Which baggage`
      `what happened` IS A `What happened to my baggage`
      `received` IS A DATE
      `placed at disposal` IS A DATE
      `days for damage` IS A NUMBER
      `days for delay` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `My complaint deadline`
`the Convention complaint deadline` MEANS
    CONSIDER `which baggage`
    WHEN `baggage I kept with me` THEN `the Act sets no time for a complaint about this`
    WHEN `checked baggage` THEN
        CONSIDER `what happened`
        WHEN `it was damaged`                        THEN `complain to the carrier in writing by` (`received` PLUS `days for damage`)
        WHEN `part of an item was lost or destroyed` THEN `complain to the carrier in writing by` (`received` PLUS `days for damage`)
        WHEN `it was delayed`                        THEN `complain to the carrier in writing by` (`placed at disposal` PLUS `days for delay`)
        WHEN `a whole item was lost or destroyed`    THEN `the Act sets no time for a complaint about this`

GIVEN `which baggage` IS A `Which baggage`
      `what happened` IS A `What happened to my baggage`
      `received` IS A DATE
      `should have been at disposal` IS A DATE
      `carriage ended` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `My complaint deadline`
`the Part IV complaint deadline` MEANS
    CONSIDER `which baggage`
    WHEN `baggage I kept with me` THEN
        CONSIDER `what happened`
        WHEN `it was delayed` THEN `the Act sets no time for a complaint about this`
        OTHERWISE `complain to the carrier in writing by` (`carriage ended` PLUS 3)
    WHEN `checked baggage` THEN
        CONSIDER `what happened`
        WHEN `it was damaged`                        THEN `complain to the carrier in writing by` (`received` PLUS 3)
        WHEN `part of an item was lost or destroyed` THEN `complain to the carrier in writing by` (`received` PLUS 3)
        WHEN `a whole item was lost or destroyed`    THEN `complain to the carrier in writing by` (`should have been at disposal` PLUS 21)
        WHEN `it was delayed`                        THEN `the Act sets no time for a complaint about this`

-- Montreal Art 35(1), Warsaw Art 29(1): extinguished "if an action is not brought within a period of two years, reckoned
-- from the date of arrival at the destination, or from the date on which the aircraft ought to have arrived, or from the date
-- on which the carriage stopped"; (2) "The method of calculating that period shall be determined by the law of the court".
-- s 34: within two years after arrival, or, where the aircraft did not arrive, the date it ought to have arrived or the date
-- the carriage stopped, "whichever is the later". For the Conventions this encoding takes the earlier of the two dates where
-- the aircraft did not arrive, the cautious reading (NOTES.md finding 7).
@export What is the last day on which I can sue the airline?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
      `the aircraft arrived at my destination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the aircraft arrived at your destination
      `the day it arrived` IS A DATE @desc The day the aircraft arrived at your destination (if it did)
      `the day it ought to have arrived` IS A DATE @desc The day the aircraft ought to have arrived at your destination
      `the day the carriage stopped` IS A DATE @desc The day your carriage stopped, if the aircraft never arrived
GIVETH AN `Answer` DATE
`The last day to sue the airline` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN
        IF `the aircraft arrived at my destination`
        THEN `answered` (`add years` `the day it arrived` 2)
        ELSE CONSIDER regime
             WHEN `Part IV, as domestic carriage` THEN `answered` (`add years` (`the later of` `the day it ought to have arrived` `the day the carriage stopped`) 2)
             WHEN `Part IV, as international carriage no Convention covers` THEN `answered` (`add years` (`the later of` `the day it ought to have arrived` `the day the carriage stopped`) 2)
             OTHERWISE `answered` (`add years` (`the earlier of` `the day it ought to have arrived` `the day the carriage stopped`) 2)

-- `the later of` and `the earlier of` are the daydate library's.

§§ `Damages -- ss 9, 9G, 9H, 15, 16, 32, 38, 39; Montreal Art 26; Warsaw Art 23`

-- s 32(1) (Part IV), Montreal Art 26, Warsaw Art 23: "Any provision tending to relieve the carrier of liability or to fix a
-- lower limit than that which is laid down in this Convention shall be null and void", the rest of the contract standing.
@export Is a term in my ticket that relieves the airline of liability, or sets a lower limit than the law's, valid?
GIVEN `where my journey begins and ends` IS A `Where my journey begins and ends` @desc Where your ticket puts the start and the final destination of your journey, and any agreed stopping place abroad
      `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the countries where your journey begins and ends are both States Parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention (for a return trip from Australia with a stop abroad, whether Australia is)
      `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague, 1955
      `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those countries are both parties to the 1929 Warsaw Convention
      `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid for the carriage, or are carried free by an airline or other air transport undertaking
      `the flight is commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the flight is operated by an airline or charter operator carrying passengers or cargo for hire or reward
GIVETH AN `Answer` BOOLEAN
`Is a ticket term lowering the airline's liability valid` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regime for` `where my journey begins and ends` `the countries are parties to the Montreal Convention` `the countries are parties to the Hague Protocol` `the countries are parties to the Warsaw Convention` `I am carried for reward, or free by an airline` `the flight is commercial`
    WHEN `not covered` r THEN `not covered` r
    WHEN `answered` regime THEN `answered` FALSE

-- ss 9G, 15, 38: "In assessing damages ... the following must not be taken into account to reduce the damages".
DECLARE `A payment I have received` IS ONE OF
    -- (a)
    `an insurance payout on my death or injury`
    -- (b)
    `a payment from a superannuation, provident or like fund, a friendly society, a benefit society or a trade union`
    -- (c)
    `a pension, social service benefit or repatriation benefit paid because of the death or injury`
    -- (d)
    `a home or household contents acquired by a family member because of the passenger's death`
    -- (e)
    `premiums that would have become payable on a life insurance policy had the passenger lived`
    `some other payment`

@export Can the damages the airline pays be reduced because of a payment I have received?
GIVEN `the payment` IS A `A payment I have received` @desc What the payment is
GIVETH AN `Answer` BOOLEAN
`Can my damages be reduced because of this payment` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the payment`
    WHEN `some other payment` THEN `not covered` `the Act lists the payments not to be deducted (ss 9G, 15, 38); whether another payment reduces damages is for the general law`
    OTHERWISE `answered` FALSE

-- ss 9H(2)-(4), 16(3)-(5), 39(2)-(4): the court first determines the damages "if there were no limit" and no negligence of
-- the passenger, reduces them "to the extent the court thinks just and equitable having regard to the share of the passenger
-- ... in the responsibility for the damage", and then, if the result exceeds the maximum liability, "further reduce[s] the
-- damages to the maximum liability". The damages and the share are the court's (asked as facts); the limit is in dollars,
-- converted from SDRs at judgment (s 9).
@export If I contributed to the damage, how much can I recover from the airline?
GIVEN `the damages the court assesses with no limit` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages, in dollars, the court finds would be recoverable if there were no limit and you had not been negligent
      `my share of the responsibility, as a percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which the court reduces those damages, having regard to your share in the responsibility for the damage (0 to 100)
      `the airline's maximum liability in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The airline's maximum liability, in dollars, under the Convention or the Act; 0 if there is no limit
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What I recover after contributory negligence` MEANS
    IF `the airline's maximum liability in dollars` GREATER THAN 0 AND `the reduced damages` GREATER THAN `the airline's maximum liability in dollars`
    THEN `the airline's maximum liability in dollars`
    ELSE `the reduced damages`
    WHERE
        `the reduced damages` MEANS `the damages the court assesses with no limit` TIMES (100 MINUS `my share of the responsibility, as a percentage`) DIVIDED BY 100

-- s 9: "a court must convert all relevant SDR amounts into Australian dollars, using the exchange rate published by the
-- Reserve Bank of Australia, being the rate that applies as at the day on which the court's judgment is given".
@export What is an SDR limit in Australian dollars?
GIVEN `the limit in SDRs` IS A NUMBER @desc The limit of liability in Special Drawing Rights
      `Australian dollars per SDR on the day of judgment` IS A NUMBER @desc The Reserve Bank of Australia's published rate, in Australian dollars for one SDR, on the day the court gives judgment
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The SDR limit in Australian dollars` MEANS `the limit in SDRs` TIMES `Australian dollars per SDR on the day of judgment`

§§ `A passenger's death -- ss 5(2)-(3), 9D, 12, 24, 35`

-- s 5(2): "a person is a family member of a passenger ... if ... the person: (a) is the passenger's spouse or de facto
-- partner; or (b) is a parent, step-parent or grandparent of the passenger; or (c) is a child, step-child, ward or grandchild
-- of the passenger; or (d) is a sibling, step-brother, step-sister, half-brother or half-sister of the passenger; or (e) is
-- wholly or partly dependent on the passenger for financial support and is: (i) a foster-sibling ... (ii) a foster-child ...
-- (iii) a guardian of the passenger; or (f)" a prescribed class (no regulations deposited). s 5(3): ex-nuptial and adoptive
-- relationships count.
DECLARE `How I was related to the passenger` IS ONE OF
    `spouse or de facto partner`
    `parent, step-parent or grandparent`
    `child, step-child, ward or grandchild`
    `sibling, step-brother, step-sister, half-brother or half-sister`
    `foster-sibling, foster-child or guardian`
    `none of these`

GIVEN `relationship` IS A `How I was related to the passenger`
      `dependent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am a family member of the passenger` IF
    CONSIDER `relationship`
    WHEN `none of these`                            THEN FALSE
    WHEN `foster-sibling, foster-child or guardian` THEN `dependent`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- ss 9D(3), (6); 12(3), (6); 35(3), (6) (s 24 applies s 12 to Part III): the liability "is enforceable for the benefit of any
-- of the passenger's family members who sustained damage because of the passenger's death", and the one action "must be for
-- the benefit of all people for whose benefit the liability is enforceable who: (a) are resident in Australia; or (b) not
-- being resident in Australia, express the desire to take the benefit of the action". The same in every Part.
@export Is the action against the airline for my relative's death on a flight for my benefit?
GIVEN `how I was related to the passenger` IS A `How I was related to the passenger` @desc How you were related to the passenger who died (relationships by adoption or outside marriage count)
      `I depended on the passenger for financial support` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were wholly or partly dependent on the passenger for financial support
      `I suffered damage because of the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you sustained damage (loss) because of the passenger's death
      `I live in Australia, or have asked to share in the action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are resident in Australia or, if not, have expressed the desire to take the benefit of the action
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the death action for my benefit` IF
        `I am a family member of the passenger` `how I was related to the passenger` `I depended on the passenger for financial support`
    AND `I suffered damage because of the death`
    AND `I live in Australia, or have asked to share in the action`
